; P9 M0 -- tile map feasibility measurement.
; See docs/tilemap-scrolling-design.md section 3.1.
;
;   load "p9m0
;   p9m0
;
; This file measures items 1 and 4 of the gate: the wall time of one present,
; and whether sound survives sustained full-screen presenting.  Items 2 and 3
; -- the game frame bodies and the before-numbers -- live in the games
; themselves, as `p9m0.checkrun` and `p9m0.trails`.
;
; The design's claim is that a scrolled frame is bound by the SPI wire, not
; the CPU: every frame repaints the whole viewport, so the present cost is
; the budget.  Measuring it needs no tile code at all -- manual refresh mode
; and `ticks` are enough today.
;
; Method: a present only sends tiles that are dirty, so each pass has to dirty
; the band first.  The dirtying is timed a second time on its own and
; subtracted, leaving the presents.  `ticks` is whole milliseconds and one
; present is ~20 of them, so the passes are repeated and averaged.

make "p9.reps 20

; Repaint the leftmost :cols tile columns (16 px each) over the full height of
; the graphics screen, marking exactly 20 x :cols tiles dirty.  A 16-pixel pen
; is centred 8 px inside each edge, so the round caps land on the band's edge
; rather than past it, and 20 strokes cover every row.
;
; Requires `window` boundary mode: under the default `wrap` the left cap of a
; stroke spills round to the right edge and dirties the whole tile row, which
; would make every band measure as a full screen.
to p9sweep :cols :colour
  local "right
  make "right (-153 + ((:cols - 1) * 16))
  setpc :colour
  repeat 20 [
    pu setx -152 sety (152 - ((repcount - 1) * 16))
    pd setx :right pu
  ]
end

; Milliseconds for one present of a 20 x :cols tile band.
to p9measure :cols
  local "t0 local "both local "paint
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat :p9.reps [p9sweep :cols (64 + (remainder repcount 2)) refresh]
  make "both (ticks - :t0)
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat :p9.reps [p9sweep :cols (64 + (remainder repcount 2))]
  make "paint (ticks - :t0)
  refresh
  output ((:both - :paint) / :p9.reps)
end

; Item 1: present wall time, for the whole screen and for the two viewport
; widths the design cares about.  320 px is the full graphics screen, 256 px
; is Checkpoint Run's road view (section 10), 64 px is a strip for the slope.
to p9present
  local "w20 local "w16 local "w4 local "slope
  cs fs ht window
  setrefresh "manual
  setpensize 16
  make "w4 (p9measure 4)
  make "w16 (p9measure 16)
  make "w20 (p9measure 20)
  setrefresh "auto
  wrap cs ts st
  make "slope ((:w20 - :w4) / 16)
  pr [P9 M0 (1) -- present wall time]
  pr []
  pr (se [full screen  320x320 :] :w20 [ms])
  pr (se [road view    256x320 :] :w16 [ms])
  pr (se [strip         64x320 :] :w4 [ms])
  pr []
  pr (se [per tile column (16x320):] :slope [ms])
  pr (se [fixed cost per present   :] (:w20 - (20 * :slope)) [ms])
  pr []
  pr (se [left of a 40 ms frame, full screen:] (40 - :w20))
  pr (se [left of a 40 ms frame, road view  :] (40 - :w16))
end

; Item 4: the P8 sound ring is serviced from an interrupt that stays enabled
; during a blit.  A scale plays while the screen presents flat out; the ear is
; the instrument here.  The screen churns for exactly as long as the voice has
; something to say -- a phrase at a time, so `play` never blocks on a full
; queue and stops the very presenting under test.
to p9audio
  cs fs ht window
  setrefresh "manual
  setpensize 16
  repeat 3 [
    play [t120 o4 l8 c d e f g a b c5 b a g f e d c]
    until [not playing?] [p9sweep 20 65 refresh]
  ]
  stopsound
  setrefresh "auto
  wrap cs ts st
  pr [P9 M0 (4) -- audio under sustained presenting]
  pr []
  pr [PASS if the scale ran evenly all the way]
  pr [through: no gaps, clicks, stuck notes or]
  pr [changes of tempo while the screen churned.]
end

to p9m0
  p9present
  pr []
  pr [--- press a key for the audio check ---]
  ignore rc
  p9audio
  pr []
  pr [Now run the games' own timings:]
  pr [  load "checkrun then p9m0.checkrun]
  pr [  load "trails   then p9m0.trails]
end
